.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jukebox
adds, lists, searches, checks, reorganizes and tags the tracks of a music
library, and syncs it with remotes.
.PP
A library is a directory.
Every file under it whose name ends in
.BR .flac ,
.B .ogg
or
.B .opus
(in any case) and does not start with a dot is a track, however deeply it is
nested.
Other files are ignored.
.PP
The library is set once with
.B jukebox init
and stored in the config file, so later commands find it on their own.
Commands that use it accept
.B \-\-library
to use a different one for a single run.
.B tag
works on the paths it is given and does not use the library at all.
.PP
Flags may be written with one or two dashes, and may come before, after or
between the command's other arguments.
An argument
.B \-\-
ends the flags: every argument after it is taken as it is, even one that
starts with a dash.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS init
.B jukebox init
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-codec
.IR name ]
.I dir
.PP
Make
.I dir
the library.
A leading
.B ~
is expanded and the path is made absolute, so the stored library does not
depend on the directory
.B jukebox
is later run from.
.PP
The library's settings file is created if it is missing, then
.I dir
is written to the config file; other lines in the config file are kept.
.B init
prints where both were stored and how many tracks it found,
and warns if there were none.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace a different library that is already set.
Without it,
.B init
refuses to change the library.
Running it again with the same
.I dir
is always allowed.
.TP
.BI \-\-codec " name"
The codec the library stores new tracks in:
.BR flac ,
.B opus
or
.BR vorbis .
A new settings file gets
.B flac
unless this flag is given;
an existing settings file is only changed when it is.
.B add
converts lossless tracks to this codec.
.SS add
.B jukebox add
.RB [ \-\-library
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.IR path ...
.PP
Copy every audio file in the given files and directories into the library as
.PP
.RS
.IR genre / "album artist" / album / "NN" ". " title . ext
.RE
.PP
using
.B ARTIST
when
.B ALBUMARTIST
is not set.
The track number has at least two digits.
If any track in the same source directory has a disc number above 1,
the file name starts with the disc number, as in
.BR "2\-03. Title.flac" .
A
.B /
in a tag becomes
.BR _ ,
and leading dots and surrounding spaces are removed.
The source files are never changed.
.PP
FLAC files are converted with
.BR ffmpeg (1)
when the library's codec is
.B opus
or
.BR vorbis .
Files that are already lossy are copied as they are, with a warning if their
codec differs from the library's, since converting them again would lose
quality.
.PP
WAV files are always converted, to FLAC when the library's codec is
.BR flac ,
since a library does not store them.
Their title, artist, album artist, album, genre, date, track and disc number
are read from the ID3 tag (version 2.3 or 2.4) if the file has one.
The RIFF INFO chunk supplies what the ID3 tag lacks, except album artist and
disc number, which it cannot hold; an INFO value that is not UTF\-8 counts as
missing.
Other fields are not kept.
.PP
When standard input is a terminal,
.B add
asks for tags the path needs and that are missing:
genre, album artist and album once for each source directory,
and artist, title and track number for each track.
A file name such as
.B "03 \- Title.wav"
supplies the track number and title in brackets.
Press Enter to accept the value in brackets.
The answers are written to the copy in the library.
Otherwise, tracks without those tags are reported and skipped.
.PP
Before copying, the tracks are checked together with those already in their
album directories, using the rules of
.BR check .
Any problem stops
.B add
before it copies anything.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print each source, its destination and whether it would be copied or converted.
Nothing is asked or written.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Add the tracks even if the check finds problems,
and replace files that already exist in the library.
Without it, an existing file is reported and left alone.
.SS list
.B jukebox list
.RB [ \-\-library
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-artist
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-album
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-genre
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-stats ]
.PP
Print the tracks of the library, one per line, as
.PP
.RS
.IR artist " \(em " album " \(em " track ". " title
.RE
.PP
Tracks are sorted by album artist (or artist, when the album artist is
missing, so a compilation stays together), then album, disc number and track
number.
Text is compared ignoring case.
.PP
.BR \-\-artist ,
.B \-\-album
and
.B \-\-genre
keep only the tracks whose tag is exactly
.IR text ,
ignoring case.
Several filters may be combined; a track must match all of them.
.PP
.B \-\-stats
prints a summary of those tracks instead of the tracks themselves:
how many tracks, albums, artists and genres there are,
the tracks per decade, the ten biggest artists and genres,
and how many albums lack cover art and tracks lack a year.
.PP
Files whose tags cannot be read are reported on standard error and skipped.
.SS search
.B jukebox search
.RB [ \-\-library
.IR path ]
.IR text ...
.PP
Print the tracks whose artist, album or title contains
.IR text ,
ignoring case.
Several arguments are joined with spaces into one search text, so quoting is
not needed.
Output, order and handling of unreadable files are the same as for
.BR list .
.SS check
.B jukebox check
.RB [ \-\-library
.IR path ]
.PP
Look for mistakes in the tags of the library and print one line for each
problem, as
.PP
.RS
.IR path ": " rule ": " message
.RE
.PP
followed by a summary of how many tracks were scanned and how many problems
were found.
The exit status is 1 if there was at least one problem.
.PP
Tracks are grouped into albums by the directory they are in;
tracks without an
.B ALBUM
tag do not belong to any album.
The rules are:
.TP
.B unreadable
The file's tags could not be read.
The file is left out of the other checks.
.TP
.B missing\-tag
A track has no
.BR ARTIST ,
.BR ALBUM ,
.B TITLE
or
.B TRACKNUMBER
tag, its
.B TRACKNUMBER
is not a number, or a track that belongs to an album has no
.BR ALBUMARTIST .
.TP
.B duplicate\-track
Two tracks in the same album have the same disc and track number.
The message names the track that had it first.
.TP
.B missing\-track
A disc of an album does not have every track up to its highest track number.
The tracks of a disc are counted from 1, or from where the disc before it
ended in an album that numbers its tracks straight through.
The path is the album's directory.
.TP
.B inconsistent\-tag
A track's
.BR ALBUM ,
.B ALBUMARTIST
or year differs from the one most tracks of its album use.
Tracks without the tag are not compared.
.SS reorganize
.B jukebox reorganize
.RB [ \-\-library
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RI [ path ...]
.PP
Move the tracks of the library to where
.B add
would put them,
.PP
.RS
.IR genre / "album artist" / album / "NN" ". " title . ext
.RE
.PP
named by the same rules.
Tracks are only moved, never rewritten or converted;
only their extension is changed to lower case.
With
.IR path s,
only the tracks among the given files and directories are moved.
The paths must be inside the library.
.PP
Tracks in the same directory with the same
.B ALBUM
are an album and stay together:
the album goes under the genre and album artist (or artist) that most of its
tracks have, and each track whose tag differs is reported in a warning.
If any track that ends up in an album's directory has a disc number above 1,
every file name there starts with the disc number,
so an album kept in one directory per disc is joined into one.
.PP
A track without a
.BR GENRE ,
.BR ALBUM ,
.B TITLE
or
.B TRACKNUMBER
tag, or without both
.B ALBUMARTIST
and
.BR ARTIST ,
is reported and left where it is, and the exit status is 1.
A track whose tags cannot be read is left where it is with a warning.
.PP
Other files in a track's directory, such as its cover file, move with the
tracks when all of that directory's tracks go to the same place;
otherwise they stay, with a warning.
Files whose names start with a dot, subdirectories and the files in the root
of the library never move this way.
.PP
Nothing is moved if two files would end up with the same path, ignoring case,
or a file would replace one that is already there; the collisions are
reported and the exit status is 1.
Otherwise each file is first moved to a temporary name in its new directory;
if that fails for any file, the files moved so far are put back and nothing
is moved.
Directories left empty are removed.
.PP
Each move is printed as
.PP
.RS
.BI moved " from " \-> " to"
.RE
.PP
with paths relative to the library, followed by how many files were moved.
The
.B .m3u8
playlists in the root of the library, such as those written by
.BR "playlist pull" ,
are updated to the tracks' new paths, and each one changed is printed.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print each move as
.BI move " from " \-> " to"
and the playlists that would be updated, but do not change anything.
.SS transcode
.B jukebox transcode
.RB [ \-\-library
.IR path ]
.I src dst
.PP
Encode the audio of
.I src
into
.I dst
with the library's codec, using
.BR ffmpeg (1).
Tags are copied; other streams, such as cover art, are dropped.
.I dst
is overwritten if it exists.
The library is only read for its codec;
.I src
and
.I dst
may be anywhere.
.SS remote
.B jukebox remote add
.RB [ \-\-insecure ]
.RB [ \-\-subsonic
.IR url ]
.I name url
.br
.B jukebox remote list
.br
.B jukebox remote remove
.I name
.PP
Manage the remotes that
.B sync
works with.
They are stored in the library's settings file.
.I name
may contain letters, digits,
.B \-
and
.BR _ .
.I url
is one of
.PP
.RS
.EX
ftp://\fR[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR][/\fIdir\fR]
ftps://\fR[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR][/\fIdir\fR]
sftp://\fIuser\fR@\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR][/\fIdir\fR]
file://\fIdir\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B ftps
uses explicit TLS (AUTH TLS) on the normal FTP port.
Without a user, the login is anonymous.
Without a
.IR dir ,
the directory the server logs in to is used.
.B sftp
connects over SSH, by default on port 22.
It logs in with the keys in the SSH agent, then the unencrypted keys
.IR ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 ,
.I id_ecdsa
and
.IR id_rsa ,
and only then asks for a password, if the server allows one.
The server's host key must already be in
.IR ~/.ssh/known_hosts ;
connect once with
.BR ssh (1)
to add it.
.B file
is a local directory, such as a mounted SD card.
The URL may not contain a password, since the settings file travels with the
library; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.PP
.B add
replaces a remote of the same name.
.B remove
also deletes its sync snapshot, so a later remote of that name starts afresh.
.TP
.B \-\-insecure
Do not verify the server's TLS certificate, for example one that is
self-signed, or, for
.BR sftp ,
its SSH host key.
.TP
.BI \-\-subsonic " url"
The Subsonic server, such as gonic, that serves the remote's tracks, as
.BR http:// \fIuser\fR@\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]
or
.BR https:// \fIuser\fR@\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR].
The user is required, since the Subsonic API has no anonymous access.
.B playlist
needs it.
.SS sync
.B jukebox sync
.RB [ \-\-library
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-prefer
.BR local | remote ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.I name
.PP
Make the library and the remote
.I name
hold the same tracks, in both directions.
Only tracks are synced; other files and the
.B .jukebox
directory are left alone on both sides.
Each action is printed as
.BR push ,
.BR pull ,
.B delete local
or
.B delete remote
followed by the track's path.
.PP
After each sync,
.B sync
records the size and modification time of every track on each side.
The next sync compares each side with its own record:
a track added or changed on one side is copied to the other,
and a track deleted on one side is deleted on the other.
The two sides' times are never compared with each other,
so the clocks of the computer and the server need not agree.
On the first sync nothing is deleted; tracks found on only one side are
copied, and tracks on both sides with the same size are taken as the same.
.PP
A track that changed on both sides, or changed on one and was deleted on the
other, is a conflict.
Conflicts are printed on standard error and left alone, and the exit status
is 1.
.PP
Copies are written under a temporary name and renamed when complete,
so an interrupted sync never leaves a partial track.
The record is saved as the sync goes, so running it again continues where it
stopped.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be done without changing anything.
.TP
.BR "\-\-prefer local" | remote
Settle conflicts in favour of that side: its version is copied over the
other, or, if it deleted the track, the track is deleted on the other side.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Allow a sync that would delete more than 5 tracks and more than a tenth of
the library on one side.
Without it,
.B sync
refuses, since that usually means the remote is empty or not mounted.
.SS playlist
.B jukebox playlist add
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-scan ]
.I remote playlist
.IR path ...
.br
.B jukebox playlist remove
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.I remote playlist
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B jukebox playlist list
.I remote
.br
.B jukebox playlist show
.I remote playlist
.br
.B jukebox playlist pull
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.I remote
.RI [ playlist ...]
.PP
Manage the playlists on the Subsonic server of the remote
.IR remote ,
set with
.BR "remote add \-\-subsonic" .
The server must serve the same tracks as the library, as it does after a
.BR sync ,
since tracks are matched by their path relative to the library.
Playlist names are matched ignoring case;
it is an error if several playlists on the server have the name.
.TP
.B add
Add every track among the given files and directories to
.IR playlist ,
creating it if it does not exist.
The paths must be inside the library.
Tracks already in the playlist are reported and left alone.
If the server does not know some of the tracks yet, it is asked to scan its
library first, and
.B add
waits for the scan to finish.
Tracks it still does not know are reported and nothing is changed;
.B sync
them to the remote first.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would change, but do not change the playlist.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-scan
Do not ask the server to scan for tracks it does not know.
.RE
.TP
.B remove
Remove from
.I playlist
every track that is one of the given paths or under one of the given
directories.
The paths must be inside the library, but need not exist, so tracks that
were moved or deleted can be removed.
A path that matches nothing in the playlist is an error.
Without any
.IR path ,
the whole playlist is deleted.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be removed or deleted, but do not change anything.
.RE
.TP
.B list
Print the server's playlists, sorted by name, one per line:
the name, a tab and the number of tracks, followed in brackets by
.B public
if the playlist is public and by its comment, if it has one.
.TP
.B show
Print the path of every track in
.IR playlist ,
relative to the library, in playlist order.
Tracks missing from the library are marked
.BR "(not in library)" .
.TP
.B pull
Save the server's playlists, or only the named ones, in the root of the
library as
.IR name .m3u8
files: extended M3U playlists whose paths are relative to the library, so
players reading the library find the tracks.
Characters that cannot be in a file name, such as
.B /
and
.BR : ,
become
.BR _ .
Existing files are replaced; files that would not change are reported as
.B unchanged
and left alone.
When two playlists would be saved in the same file, ignoring case, only the
first is written and the other is reported as an error.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the files that would be written, but do not write them.
.RE
.PP
The server keeps counting the tracks of a playlist whose files were moved or
deleted, but leaves them out of the playlist.
.BR show ,
.B pull
and
.B list
therefore may disagree on how many tracks a playlist has;
.B show
and
.B pull
say so on standard error.
Add the moved tracks again with
.BR "playlist add" .
.SS "tag show"
.B jukebox tag show
.IR path ...
.PP
For every track among the given files and directories, print its path
followed by its artist, album artist, album, title, disc, track, year and
genre, then its lyrics, one indented line per line of text.
Tags that are not set are left out.
.SS "tag set"
.B jukebox tag set
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-artist
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-album
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-title
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-genre
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-track
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-year
.IR n ]
.IR path ...
.PP
Change the tags of every track among the given files and directories.
Only the tags whose flags are given are changed; every other tag is kept.
Giving a flag an empty value, as in
.BR "\-\-genre \(dq\(dq" ,
removes that tag.
At least one tag flag is required.
.PP
Each file is rewritten to a temporary file next to it, which then replaces
the original, so an interrupted write never leaves a damaged track.
The path of each updated file is printed.
.TP
.BI \-\-artist " text"
Set
.BR ARTIST .
.TP
.BI \-\-album " text"
Set
.BR ALBUM .
.TP
.BI \-\-title " text"
Set
.BR TITLE .
.TP
.BI \-\-genre " text"
Set
.BR GENRE .
.TP
.BI \-\-track " n"
Set
.BR TRACKNUMBER .
.TP
.BI \-\-year " n"
Set
.BR DATE .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the files that would be updated, but do not change them.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Allow
.B \-\-title
or
.B \-\-track
when the paths hold more than one track.
Without it the command is refused, since every track would get the same
title or number, which is almost always a mistake.
.SS "tag art"
.B jukebox tag art show
.IR path ...
.br
.B jukebox tag art set
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.I image
.IR path ...
.br
.B jukebox tag art extract
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.I file
.RI [ out ]
.br
.B jukebox tag art remove
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.IR path ...
.PP
Manage cover art: the front cover embedded in each track, and the cover file
of an album, named
.BR cover.jpg ,
.B cover.jpeg
or
.B cover.png
(in any case) and kept in the album's directory.
A track with no picture marked as a front cover uses a picture of type
\(lqother\(rq instead, which is how
.BR ffmpeg (1)
embeds covers.
Other embedded pictures are always kept.
.PP
The cover file only goes with the tracks of a directory given as a
.IR path :
naming single tracks never touches the cover file their album shares.
Files are rewritten the same way as by
.BR "tag set" ,
and every file written or removed is printed.
.TP
.B show
For every track among the given files and directories, print its path and
its front cover's format, size in pixels and size in bytes, or
.BR none .
.TP
.B set
Embed the JPEG or PNG
.I image
as the front cover of every track among the paths, and make it the cover file
of each directory holding them, named
.B cover.jpg
or
.BR cover.png ;
other cover files there are removed.
A warning is printed when
.I image
is larger than 1 MB, since every track grows by its size.
Tracks and cover files that already hold the image are left alone.
Nothing is changed if any track already has a different front cover, a
directory already has a different cover file, or a directory
.I path
holds the tracks of more than one album directory.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would change, but do not change it.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace existing front covers and cover files, and allow a directory holding
several albums, which all get the same cover.
.RE
.TP
.B extract
Write the front cover of the track
.I file
to
.IR out .
When
.I out
is a directory or is not given (meaning the directory of
.IR file ),
the cover is written there as
.B cover.jpg
or
.BR cover.png .
It is an error if
.I file
has no front cover.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace
.I out
if it exists and holds a different image.
.RE
.TP
.B remove
Remove the front cover of every track among the paths, and the cover files of
each directory holding them.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would change, but do not change it.
.RE
.SS "Paths and errors in tag"
A
.I path
given to
.BR "tag show" ,
.BR "tag set" ,
.B "tag art show"
or
.B "tag art remove"
may be a track or a directory, which is searched the same way as the library.
A path that cannot be read or holds no tracks is an error.
An error on one file does not stop the others:
all errors are printed at the end and the exit status is 1.
.SS version
.B jukebox version
.PP
Print the version of
.BR jukebox .
.SH TAGS
.B jukebox
reads and writes Vorbis comments, the tag format of FLAC, Ogg Vorbis and
Opus files.
It uses these tags:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l.
ARTIST	artist of the track
ALBUMARTIST	artist of the whole album
ALBUM	album title
TITLE	track title
TRACKNUMBER	track number, as \fIn\fR or \fIn\fR/\fItotal\fR
DISCNUMBER	disc number, as \fIn\fR or \fIn\fR/\fItotal\fR
DATE	release date; its first four characters are the year
GENRE	genre
LYRICS	lyrics, shown by \fBtag show\fR
.TE
.RE
.PP
Tag names are matched ignoring case.
When a tag appears more than once, the first value is used.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-library " path"
Use
.I path
as the library instead of the one in the config file.
A leading
.B ~
is expanded.
Accepted by
.BR add ,
.BR list ,
.BR search ,
.BR check ,
.BR reorganize ,
.BR transcode ,
.BR remote ,
.B sync
and
.BR playlist .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Without a command, or as the command
.BR help ,
print the list of commands.
After a command, print that command's flags.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/commonjukebox/config
The user's config file, by default
.IR ~/.config/commonjukebox/config .
It is written by
.BR "jukebox init" .
Its only key is
.BR library ,
the path of the library.
.TP
.IB library /.jukebox/config
The library's settings file.
It is kept inside the library so the settings travel with it.
Its keys are
.BR codec ,
which is
.BR flac ,
.B opus
or
.B vorbis
.RB ( flac
when unset);
.BI ffmpeg. codec\fR,
the encoder options
.B add
and
.B transcode
pass to
.BR ffmpeg (1)
for that codec, separated by spaces and without quoting, such as
.B "ffmpeg.opus = \-b:a 128k \-vbr on"
(they replace the built\-in
.BR "\-compression_level 8" ,
.B "\-b:a 160k \-vbr on"
and
.BR "\-q:a 6" ;
empty or unset keeps them);
and, for each remote,
.BI remote. name .url\fR,
.BI remote. name .insecure
(\fBtrue\fR or unset) and
.BI remote. name .subsonic\fR,
written by
.BR "jukebox remote add" .
.TP
.IB library /.jukebox/sync/ name
The record of the last sync with the remote
.IR name .
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/known_hosts
Host keys of SFTP servers.
.TP
.I ~/.netrc
Passwords for FTP servers, in the usual
.BR machine / login / password
format.
.PP
Both config files have one
.IB key " = " value
setting per line.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored, as are unknown keys.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B JUKEBOX_PASSWORD
The password for a remote whose URL has a user,
and for a remote's Subsonic server.
For
.BR sftp ,
it is only used when SSH keys were not accepted.
When it is not set,
.B sync
and
.B playlist
look in
.IR ~/.netrc ,
then ask on the terminal.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the config file is looked for.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed,
.B check
found problems,
.B reorganize
left tracks without the tags it needs, or
.B sync
skipped conflicts.
.TP
.B 2
No command, an unknown command or an invalid flag.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make
.I ~/Music
the library and store new tracks as Opus:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox init \-\-codec opus ~/Music
.EE
.RE
.PP
List one album, then search the whole library:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox list \-\-artist Radiohead \-\-album "OK Computer"
jukebox search paranoid android
.EE
.RE
.PP
Look for missing tags and tracks:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox check
.EE
.RE
.PP
Move the tracks of one directory into place after fixing their tags,
trying it out first:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox reorganize \-\-dry\-run ~/Music/unsorted
jukebox reorganize ~/Music/unsorted
.EE
.RE
.PP
Tag a downloaded album, trying it out first, and look at the result:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox tag set \-\-dry\-run \-\-artist Radiohead \-\-year 1997 ~/Downloads/okc
jukebox tag set \-\-artist Radiohead \-\-year 1997 ~/Downloads/okc
jukebox tag show ~/Downloads/okc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give the album a cover, then check it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox tag art set ~/Downloads/folder.jpg ~/Downloads/okc
jukebox tag art show ~/Downloads/okc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove the genre from one track:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox tag set \-\-genre "" ~/Music/okc/01.flac
.EE
.RE
.PP
Sync the library with a phone running an FTP server, trying it out first:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox remote add phone ftp://me@192.168.1.20:2121/Music
jukebox sync \-\-dry\-run phone
jukebox sync phone
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a remote served by gonic a playlist, then save its playlists in the
library:
.PP
.RS
.EX
jukebox remote add \-\-subsonic https://me@music.example.org nas sftp://me@nas/music
jukebox sync nas
jukebox playlist add nas Favourites ~/Music/Rock/Radiohead/"OK Computer"
jukebox playlist show nas Favourites
jukebox playlist pull nas
.EE
.RE
